{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OBSERVATION\nThe ticket reports a commit prepared with `git commit -F /tmp/commitmsg.txt` after a concurrent session wrote the same fixed path, so the commit acquired the other session's message. Current guidance (`AGENTS.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`) warns about `make amend-msg` preserving Change-Id but does not tell authors where to stage commit-message files. `Makefile:amend-msg` passes `FILE` through to `scripts/amend_commit_message.py`, and `_read_message_file` only checks that the path exists and is non-empty. There is no guard against fixed shared scratch paths. A sweep also found other shared-tmp patterns, e.g. `scripts/install-gerrit-hook.sh` uses `mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rebar-commit-msg.XXXXXX\"` (unique) while the bad reported path was fixed and non-unique.\n\nHYPOTHESIS\nThe root mechanism is a shared-path TOCTOU/collision, not a Git failure: `git commit -F <path>` reads whatever bytes are at `<path>` at commit time and has no ownership/session provenance check. A fixed path under host-shared scratch is writable by another same-user agent session, so the last writer wins silently; Git exits 0 because the file is valid, even though the message semantically belongs to a different diff.\n\nCONFIRMATION\nI reproduced the mechanism in a scratch directory (not `/tmp`): initialized a repo, staged a change, wrote `Intended message` to a shared `commitmsg.txt`, overwrote that same file with `Other session message` to simulate a second session, and ran `git commit -F <shared-file>`. `git log -1 --format=%B` showed `Other session message`. This would have been disproven if Git captured the first content earlier, detected an external overwrite, or warned about message provenance; it did none of those.\n\nSUGGESTED FIX DIRECTION\nRecord guidance that commit-message bodies and all scratch files must be per-session/per-job unique, never fixed shared paths. Add a low-noise guard in `make amend-msg`/`scripts/amend_commit_message.py` that rejects or warns on fixed shared scratch paths, while allowing repo/session-local files and genuinely unique `mktemp`-style paths. Sweep fixed `/tmp` documentation/scripts separately and classify unique templates vs collision-prone fixed names."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788737324670372001,"uuid":"7d7e7494-3ade-423a-b8fe-35929bb0bcc7"}